{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793697789,"nonce":"wOUk51sWQCEuhcWbUILj+3gpe/M=","title":"Flow shaders leave CSS: a compile-time WGSL macro, drawn by the canvas element","message":"Owner decision, 2026-11-03: `@flow` was a good experiment and should live as a macro (the existing `flow!`) that transpiles to WGSL at compile time, used in the canvas element. Keep pointer-query, which cuts the Rust logic of pointer-driven effects. Part of the convergence in edadd7d: @flow is a Blinc-only at-rule that the ABI engine, ashui and blinc_ts do not have.\n\nWhat exists (read, counted)\n- The IR is blinc_core::flow (4.7k lines: FlowGraph, FlowExpr, FlowFunc, FlowTarget and the built-in steps).\n- The CSS parser is blinc_css parser/flow (1.4k lines, plus 221 lines of tests), on blinc_css's shared nom helpers; Stylesheet keeps parsed flows (flows, get_flow, contains_flow, flow_names, flow_count) and the CSS side reads a `flow:` property.\n- WGSL codegen is blinc_gpu flow_codegen (2.0k lines), run at run time through FlowPipeline (flow_pipeline.rs, 0.8k), cached by the renderer.\n- Rendering: blinc_app motion_rendering draws each flow element over its own SDF base, finding the graph in the element (`div().flow(graph)`, Div.flow_name, Div.flow_graph) or by name in the stylesheet.\n- The `flow!` macro is a macro_rules in blinc_css that stringifies its body and parses it at run time through the CSS @flow parser. It is not a compile-time transpiler; blinc_macros has no WGSL or flow code.\n- DrawContext (blinc_core) has no shader call, so a canvas cannot draw a flow today.\n- Users: examples flow_demo and semantic_flow_demo, the book chapter advanced/flow-shaders.md (607 lines), the DSL grammar and one DSL stylesheet test (@flow), pointer_query.rs docs.\n\nTarget\n- flow! is a proc macro. It parses its body at compile time, emits the WGSL and a descriptor (name, target, inputs such as uv, time, pointer, and their types) as a const FlowShader. Nothing is parsed or generated at run time.\n- A canvas draws a FlowShader: a DrawContext extension (draw_shader over the canvas bounds with the shader's inputs), and a blinc_gpu pipeline built from the raw WGSL.\n- Pointer inputs come from pointer-query, as the --pointer values and pointer spaces do today; pointer-query stays in blinc_css (it is 54 lines there and 738 in blinc_layout) and its docs stop naming @flow.\n- A small pure crate holds the transpiler (parser and codegen over the IR) so the proc macro can run it without wgpu: blinc_macros depends on it, blinc_gpu keeps only the pipeline. The IR stays in blinc_core or moves with it; decide when the crate is cut.\n\nSteps, each landing alone\nF1. Take @flow out of CSS, keeping programmatic flows working. Move the parser into the transpiler crate with its tests; blinc_css stops reading @flow blocks, the `flow:` property and Stylesheet's flow accessors; flow! (moved with it) and div().flow(graph) work as before; motion_rendering keeps drawing graphs held by the element and drops the stylesheet lookup. Gate: the moved tests, flow_demo and semantic_flow_demo unchanged, the blinc_css ledger unchanged.\nF2. Move codegen (flow_codegen) into the same crate and make flow! a proc macro emitting WGSL and the descriptor at compile time. Gate: the generated WGSL equals what run-time codegen produced for the existing demos (compare text), and the WGSL validates (naga) in a test.\nF3. DrawContext::draw_shader and the canvas path: a pipeline from raw WGSL, the inputs bound per frame, pointer from pointer-query. Gate: a headless recorder golden of a canvas drawing a FlowShader, with a pointer input.\nF4. Remove the rest: div().flow(), flow_name, flow_graph, FlowRef, the element draw path in motion_rendering, run-time codegen and FlowPipeline's graph entry; port the two examples and the book chapter to the canvas form.\nF5. DSL: the @flow mention in the grammar and the dsl_core stylesheet test (DSL work stays out of 0.6.0).\n\nOpen questions for the owner\n- F1 before F2 and F3 leaves flow elements drawing as they do now while the macro is still run-time; removing the element path (F4) waits for the canvas path. Acceptable, or should the element path go with F1?\n- Where the IR lives: blinc_core (the transpiler crate depends on it) or the transpiler crate (blinc_core re-exports).\n- A crate name for the transpiler, and whether it also serves ashui and blinc_ts canvases (blinc_ts has a canvas shader library of its own).","files":null}]}